<node id="Heading Hierarchy">
  <data key="d0">Heading Hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Heading Hierarchy (H4, H3, etc.) determines the weight of links, with higher-level headings carrying more weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Heading hierarchy is the ordered structure of headings (H1, H2, H3, etc.) used to organize content and internal linking.&lt;SEP&gt;Heading Hierarchy refers to the structural organization of headings in content, also known as Contextual Hierarchy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-043bc92a7015e647914eacacd758a036&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a388368023eee5caf3dbbc03fcdd278d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6ee67e32c07c0182dd83e65b717d89d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779795644</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Red Color">
  <data key="d0">Red Color</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">In the contextual connection, Red Color signals that an internal link is placed but the target article brief is not read or written, or it is skipped.</data>
