<node id="Contextual Border">
  <data key="d0">Contextual Border</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Contextual Border is a conceptual and structural boundary within written content that delineates the macro context from the micro context. In article construction, it separates the main, overarching content—often addressing primary topics or broad questions—from supplementary or detailed content that provides deeper, specific information. This demarcation ensures a clear transition between the general narrative and supporting material, helping to organize content into distinct sections.

As a structural element, the Contextual Border is frequently implemented using heading markers such as "1H2" to visually and hierarchically partition sections. Beyond its physical placement, the term also describes the scope of content relevance and comprehensiveness, indicating where the focus shifts from high-level exposition to granular details. It can further serve to separate different types of questions or topics within a document, acting as a guide for both content creators and readers to navigate the layered structure of information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b39188a27a68c4df8a21ff349b5a66ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c77ed812b87a04f08a3ce700769524d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b837e1fc804bb7df2114c65c3758ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ad6b0780f4ae2de415293f3a5c7c523&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-237f0369c76d4e73efce226fd0388537&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e745285933563d1ddbb9665a9120ab7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60923d03c70551cefab5f72ed0dee9ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e516c24df7f67e764c86da19d203579</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1779807403</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
